#: src/imports.md:1
msgid "Imports"
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:3
msgid ""
"One `justfile` can include the contents of another using `import` statements."
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:5
msgid "If you have the following `justfile`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:14
msgid "And the following text in `foo/bar.just`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:21
msgid ""
"`foo/bar.just` will be included in `justfile` and recipe `b` will be defined:"
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:31
msgid ""
"The `import` path can be absolute or relative to the location of the "
"justfile containing it. A leading `~/` in the import path is replaced with "
"the current user’s home directory."
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:35
msgid ""
"Justfiles are insensitive to order, so included files can reference "
"variables and recipes defined after the `import` statement."
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:38
msgid ""
"Imported files can themselves contain `import`s, which are processed "
"recursively."
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:41
msgid ""
"`allow-duplicate-recipes` and `allow-duplicate-variables` allow duplicate "
"recipes and variables, respectively, to override each other, instead of "
"producing an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:45
msgid "Within a module, later definitions override earlier definitions:"
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:56
msgid ""
"When `import`s are involved, things unfortunately get much more complicated "
"and hard to explain."
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:59
msgid ""
"Shallower definitions always override deeper definitions, so recipes at the "
"top level will override recipes in imports, and recipes in an import will "
"override recipes in an import which itself imports those recipes."
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:63
msgid ""
"When two duplicate definitions are imported and are at the same depth, the "
"one from the earlier import will override the one from the later import."
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:66
msgid ""
"This is because `just` uses a stack when processing imports, pushing imports "
"onto the stack in source-order, and always processing the top of the stack "
"next, so earlier imports are actually handled later by the compiler."
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:70
msgid ""
"This is definitely a bug, but since `just` has very strong backwards "
"compatibility guarantees and we take enormous pains not to break anyone’s "
"`justfile`, see issue [\\#2540](https://github.com/casey/just/issues/2540) "
"for discussion on whether or not we can actually fix it."
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:75
msgid ""
"Imports may be made optional by putting a `?` after the `import` keyword:"
msgstr ""

#: src/imports.md:81
msgid ""
"Importing the same source file multiple times is not an error<sup>1.37.0</"
"sup>. This allows importing multiple justfiles, for example `foo.just` and "
"`bar.just`, which both import a third justfile containing shared recipes, "
"for example `baz.just`, without the duplicate import of `baz.just` being an "
"error:"
msgstr ""
